    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis examines the transformative relationship between Alibaba and the Chinese government, focusing on the period from 2014 to the time of writing this thesis. The study is divided into three distinct stages: the mutually supportive and engaging period (2014-2020), the regulatory crackdown phase (2021-2022), and the recent phase of cautious normalization (2023 onwards). During the first stage, Alibaba achieved significant milestones, including two of the largest IPOs in history—first on the NASDAQ in 2014 and then a planned dual listing in Shanghai and Hong Kong in 2020, which was ultimately suspended by Chinese regulators. This suspension marked the beginning of a stringent regulatory crackdown that extended beyond Alibaba to the entire private sector, significantly impacting the market value and operational strategies of major internet companies. The crackdown included a record $2.8 billion antitrust fine on Alibaba and forced restructuring of its financial arm, Ant Group. In response, Alibaba exhibited compliance by restructuring its operations, divesting from media assets, and aligning more closely with governmental expectations. The recent phase of cautious normalization reflects a strategic recalibration by the Chinese government, balancing regulatory control with economic revitalization while Alibaba shifts its operational focus back to e-commerce through holding the previous business strategy accountable and re-organization. The changing of governmental attitude reflects the internal change of goals from boosting private sector innovation to exerting higher levels of control towards the private sector, while the response from Alibaba presents a novel model of adaptability toward greater apparent uncertainty of government interests. This thesis provides a comprehensive analysis of the evolving dynamics between Alibaba and the Chinese government as a case study, highlighting the unique nature of their interactions and the broader implications for understanding the governmental-private interaction within China.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
